Key art for an ORIGINAL puzzle game named SPIRELIGHT (do not imitate any existing game's look). Landscape 16:9.
A true isometric (orthographic, about 30 degree view) diorama: one tall clockwork spire floating above a sea of soft glowing clouds at deep dusk.
The spire is assembled from cube-shaped blocks of warm weathered sandstone with carved trim bands, joined with brass and verdigris-green copper machinery: big interlocking gears, a crank wheel, pipes, a rotating bridge section, a stone staircase wrapping around the tower, small arched windows glowing amber, hanging iron lanterns, moss and ivy and tiny glowing blue flowers on ledges, a thin waterfall of mist spilling off one edge into the clouds.
At the very top: an unlit great beacon lamp with a big glass lens inside a brass cage.
On a walkway halfway up: a tiny lamplighter automaton, a small brass clockwork figure whose head is a round glass lantern with a warm flame inside, short teal scarf, thin jointed legs, wind-up key on its back.
Lighting: low warm sun from the left, rich teal-to-violet sky gradient with first stars, soft god rays, bloom on the lanterns, warm/cool contrast.
Rendering: highly detailed stylized 3D render, tilt-shift miniature feel, physically based materials (stone grain, brass sheen, glass), crisp readable silhouettes, walkable surfaces are pale paving slabs that read clearly.
Not pastel, not flat minimalist, not low-poly. No text, no logo, no UI.
